WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.034
Good evidence is current, relevant, traceable, complete enough, and tied to a specific control or assertion.

2
00:00:07.034 --> 00:00:12.933
Current evidence reflects the system as it operates now or during the period being reviewed.

3
00:00:12.933 --> 00:00:16.865
Relevant evidence supports the specific claim under review.

4
00:00:16.865 --> 00:00:24.504
Traceable evidence can be connected back to the system, component, owner, control, finding, or operational activity.

5
00:00:24.504 --> 00:00:30.705
Complete enough means the evidence lets a reviewer understand what happened without guesswork.

6
00:00:30.705 --> 00:00:32.596
Evidence can take many forms.

7
00:00:32.596 --> 00:00:36.075
Configuration records can show how systems are set up.

8
00:00:36.075 --> 00:00:37.588
Logs can show activity.

9
00:00:37.588 --> 00:00:44.243
Screenshots can be useful at a high level when they are dated, contextualized, and tied to a system state.

10
00:00:44.243 --> 00:00:47.571
Tickets can show workflow, ownership, and closure.

11
00:00:47.571 --> 00:00:50.445
Scan results can show vulnerability status.

12
00:00:50.445 --> 00:01:00.277
Meeting records, approvals, access reviews, test results, monitoring outputs, and operational records can all support different kinds of assertions.

13
00:01:00.277 --> 00:01:02.394
Weak evidence creates friction.

14
00:01:02.394 --> 00:01:05.192
Vague assurances do not show what happened.

15
00:01:05.192 --> 00:01:09.050
Stale artifacts may describe a system that no longer exists.

16
00:01:09.050 --> 00:01:13.209
Unsupported statements ask reviewers to trust without a record.

17
00:01:13.209 --> 00:01:18.125
Evidence that cannot be tied back to the system may be impossible to evaluate.

18
00:01:18.125 --> 00:01:24.856
A screenshot with no date, owner, environment, or explanation may create more questions than it answers.

19
00:01:24.856 --> 00:01:28.411
Evidence quality matters as much as evidence quantity.

20
00:01:28.411 --> 00:01:30.528
Good evidence is also protected.

21
00:01:30.528 --> 00:01:40.360
Security documentation can reveal architecture, configuration, vulnerabilities, privileged processes, dependencies, and operational practices.

22
00:01:40.360 --> 00:01:49.209
Evidence repositories need access control, version tracking, retention expectations, and handling rules appropriate to sensitivity.

23
00:01:49.209 --> 00:01:54.881
Evidence should be reviewable by the right people, but it should not be casually exposed.

24
00:01:54.881 --> 00:01:59.797
Trustworthy evidence is useful, understandable, and responsibly managed.
